-- pending: a lend held across a tick (a suspension; T1, #172): tier 2 is open and has no path
-- now: 27:11: the row of an unwinding borrow's callback has only abortive operations, of names other than 0, whose arguments outlive every lifetime and do not mention its lifetime
--
-- A name bound inside a borrow block over a loop, beside a variable of the same name outside it:
-- the block's `let mut acc` is a variable of its own (SPEC §41, rule R1), and the `acc` read
-- after the block is `main`'s.
--
-- Needs a lend held across a tick. Every loop is at the row `div` (SPEC §14), and each of its back
-- edges ticks, a control operation that resumes; the borrow of `t` at 27:11 spans the ticks of the
-- `for` over the input at 29:5. The kernel's rule for a lend at a row has only abortive operations
-- (`Row.Unwind`), so it refuses the lend. Holding a lend across a suspension is tier 2, which is
-- open and has no path.
--
-- Once it lands: on the input `1234`, `i32[105]`, `live 0`: 10 + 20 + 30 + 40, the newline's entry
-- 0, and `main`'s 5.
fn tab(i: i32) -> i32 = i * 10

def get : !(all 'a < top. imm 'a (array i32) * i32 => i32) =
  !thunkv Fun 'a < top. return (thunkv fun (p : imm 'a (array i32) * i32) ->
    let (b, i) = p;
    withload['a, 'z, array i32, i32] b (thunkv Fun 'z < dom. return (thunkv fun (xs : array_imm 'z i32) ->
      match array_get['z, i32] (xs, i) { inl e -> let v = *e; boxed['z] v | inr _ -> boxed['z] 0 })))

main -> i32 =
  let t = alloc(array_new[i32] (10, tab));
  let acc = 5;
  let r = borrow imm t { b ->
    let mut acc = 0;
    for c in bytes(&mut stdin) { acc = acc + get(b, to_i32(c) - 48) };
    acc
  };
  acc + r
